So let's get into it.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.197
Let's start talking about housing

00:01:13.197 --> 00:01:15.990
and why things are getting
better in the housing sector

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:18.060
and why maybe that's
not such a good thing.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.940
So, first charter I wanna
put up here on your screen

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.190
is the NAHB Housing Market Index.

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:26.700
Now, we actually just got the
latest update for that report,

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.220
out right now at the
start of "Market Takes,"

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:32.310
and that report came in
higher than last month.

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:34.830
Now, you see there on your
screen the last four months,

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:37.590
you see the report just
going up, up, up higher.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:39.990
Well, it went even
higher April this month.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:42.180
The last month you see
there is 44 from March,

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.030
in April it went up to 45.

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:46.710
So that index just
showing the overall pace

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:49.110
of price increases, of confidence,

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:51.150
of consumers, of builders,
and things like that,

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:52.350
and that has been picking up.

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:53.377
Now you normally think,

00:01:53.377 --> 00:01:55.650
"Okay, that's a good thing,
the economy's growing,"

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:57.960
but that might not be such a good thing

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.325
because we're seeing all of
the housing market indicators

00:02:00.325 --> 00:02:02.940
move in exactly this same direction.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:04.170
So, that chart there,

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:06.570
illustrating overall
home builder confidence,

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:09.210
and the pace of increases in prices,

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:11.250
we are seeing that
exact same kind of trend

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.410
where things have been
moving lower, lower, lower.

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:14.640
The Fed raised interest rates,

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.410
they're trying to slow down the economy.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:17.310
They want consumers

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:19.350
to reconsider their purchasing decisions,

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:20.670
not take on more credit.

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:23.160
And that's they want, they
wanna see things come down,

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:24.750
and get lower, and get worse.

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:26.100
And so there's less confidence,

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:28.050
less borrowing, less spending.

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:29.160
But that's not what we've seen

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.110
the past three months,
going all the way back.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:32.760
And now past four or five months,

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.670
all the way through December of last year,

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:37.140
we have seen a pickup and an increase,

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.270
and that continued in April.

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:41.730
Now, we're also seeing
that in housing starts,

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:45.210
we get the latest housing
starts data on Tuesday.

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:47.820
That last month accelerated 9.8%,

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:49.800
almost 10% month over month.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:52.020
That was the fastest pace in September.

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:53.025
Home construction also.

00:02:53.025 --> 00:02:55.140
Home construction had been trending lower,

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:56.880
and those housing starts number tells us

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:58.350
about what builders are doing,

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:00.600
they're actually starting
housing projects.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:04.260
That picked up in a big
way, almost 10% last month.

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.360
We will see if that continues tomorrow.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.190
A big piece of that was multi-family,

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:10.380
which we know is people putting money,

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.400
investing in units for renting out

00:03:13.453 --> 00:03:14.880
rather than for living.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:16.230
We also get the mortgage,

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:19.170
the MBA's Weekly Mortgage
Application Survey.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:22.056
Last week, the 30-year
fixed rate fell a 6.3%,

00:03:22.056 --> 00:03:23.910
the lowest level in two months.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:28.710
And we saw applications for
mortgages increase by 8%.

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:30.687
Mike Fratantoni, he is the SVB

00:03:30.687 --> 00:03:32.550
and the chief economist of the SVP.

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:34.050
Excuse me, senior vice president

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:35.430
at the Mortgage Bankers Association,

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:36.780
and their chief economist.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.390
He said that prospective
home buyers this year

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:42.360
have been quite sensitive to
any drop in mortgage rates.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:44.424
Mortgage rates dropping a 6.3%.

00:03:44.424 --> 00:03:45.330
Now, that's a little lower,

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:47.610
but remember a year or two ago

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:50.490
you saw mortgage rates down
about that three handle,

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:51.630
and we getting up to the four handle.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:54.417
Now mortgage rates, still
high, still at 6.3%,

00:03:54.417 --> 00:03:56.610
but you're seeing housing starts pick up.

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:58.770
You're seeing mortgage
applications pick up.

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:02.340
And everything the Fed is doing
to try to slow the economy

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.800
is kind of being undone,
slowly but surely.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:07.470
Remember, mortgages,
mortgage rates, housing,

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:10.800
all those things, were the
first thing to slow down

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.990
when the Fed first started
raising interest rates.

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:15.060
Now they're starting to pick back up,

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:16.531
as the Fed is expected to slow down

00:04:16.531 --> 00:04:19.110
its interest rate hiking cycle.

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:21.054
The markets are showing
one more expected increase

00:04:21.054 --> 00:04:24.750
in May next month and then
cuts starting in July.

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:26.098
Well, consumers are responding,

00:04:26.098 --> 00:04:28.200
taking on more mortgages, more debts,

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.810
and we're seeing housing
starts to pick back up.

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:32.910
We've seen the housing
market index pick back up.

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:36.360
We also will get the
existing home sales report

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:37.650
out on Thursday.

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:41.640
Last month we saw Existing
Home Sales rise by almost 15%.

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:43.230
We are gonna be right here Thursday,

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:45.840
breaking down the Existing
Home Sales report,

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:46.950
telling you why that happens.

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:48.750
That could be a big market mover

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:51.065
because existing homes
are most of the economy,

00:04:51.065 --> 00:04:53.490
or most of the market,
when it comes to mortgages.

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:56.160
So be with us right here on
"Market Takes" Thursday at noon,

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:57.360
we'll break all that down.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:59.280
Now let's talk about
earnings really quickly.

